hadiths[2]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1653","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Aziz al-Aylisi, that Salamah narrated to them, from 'Uqayl, who informed me Muhammad ibn Muslim, that Mahmud ibn al-Rabi' al-Ansari informed him, that 'Itban ibn Malik — who was one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and one of those who fought at Badr from among the Ansar — came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said:

""O Messenger of Allah, I have lost my eyesight, and I lead my people in prayer. But when the rains come, the valley between me and them becomes flooded, so I cannot reach their mosque to pray with them. I wish, O Messenger of Allah, that you would come and pray in my house so that I may take it as a place of prayer.""

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I will do so, if Allah wills.""

Ibn Malik said: So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and Abu Bakr went out early in the morning when the day had advanced. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sought permission, which I granted. He did not sit until he entered the house. Then he said: ""Where would you like me to pray in your house?""

I pointed to a place in the house. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up and said the takbir, and we lined up behind him. He prayed two rak'ahs, then he gave the salam. We seated him on a mat we had prepared for him.

Then a group of the people of the house gathered around us until a considerable number of men had assembled in the house. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Where is Malik ibn al-Dukhayshin?""

Some of them said: ""That is a hypocrite who does not love Allah or His Messenger.""

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not say that about him. Do you not see that he says: 'There is no god but Allah,' seeking thereby the Face of Allah?""

They said: ""Allah and His Messenger know best. We see his face and his loyalty to be with the hypocrites.""

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Verily, Allah has forbidden the Fire for anyone who says: 'There is no god but Allah,' seeking thereby the Face of Allah.""

Muhammad (meaning al-Zuhri) said: So I asked al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Ansari — who was one of the Banu Salim from their nobles — about the hadith of Mahmud ibn al-Rabi', and he confirmed it.

**CHAPTER:**"
"1654","###
 In the narration of Ma'mar, from Az-Zuhri, it is mentioned: ""Indeed, my eyesight has become impaired."" This phrase may refer to someone whose vision is poor, even if he can still see with difficulty; or it may indicate that he has become completely blind and sees nothing. I have no doubt that he became blind after this incident, but at the time he asked the Prophet (ﷺ), he was still able to see, though with difficulty.

It was narrated to us by Muhammad bin Yahya, from the report of"
